      "role": "user",
      "content": "Thanks. I'll try that comparative method. The problem is that even when I isolate the \"noise,\" it doesn't behave like random statistical variance. It feels... targeted. \n\nWhen I look at these specific junctions, the erosion doesn't just degrade the sound; it seems to actively displace it. For example, in certain oblique forms of \"pata,\" a specific vowel doesn't just weaken, it's almost as if it's been physically removed or replaced by a tiny, discordant glitch that doesn't belong in the phonological system. It's a very restless, jittery kind of irregularity, like these small-scale disruptions are occupying the narrow crevices of the morpheme boundary just to interfere with the root's integrity.\n\nIs there any model in morphology that accounts for this kind of \"intrusive\" or \"interstitial\" phenomenon? I'm struggling to find a way to describe these small, somewhat erratic elements that seem to specifically target and mess with the stability of the junction.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
