      "content": "I follow you on the prosodic constraints and boundary effects, but \"pressure\" still feels like a passive, mechanical description for what I'm seeing in the data. It doesn't feel like a predictable force being applied; it feels like there's a very small, very active agency at work within those gaps.\n\nWhen I look at the way these specific phonetic glitches manifest—how they seem to hide in the cracks of the stem and then suddenly emerge to \"nibble\" at a vowel or a consonant—it has a very specific, almost frantic quality. It's as if these tiny, unseen elements aren't just reacting to the junction, but are actually occupying the interstitial spaces of the word to actively disrupt its shape. \n\nIs there any way to model linguistic elements that behave as discrete, intrusive units that seem to \"lurk\" in the morpheme boundaries? I'm trying to find a way to conceptualize these small-scale, erratic disruptions that seem to have a distinct, almost predatory presence within the phonological structure.",
